A recent timing vulnerability, “EarlyBird”, demonstrates that semantic caches used in large language models (LLMs) can be exploited to infer private user information. However, the reported attack is conducted in a constrained setting in which the attacker, the victim, and the shared LLM reside on the same device, no other users access the LLM concurrently, and the demonstrated use case is limited to inferring private information about adults. In this work, we overcome these constraints by presenting a practical timing attack, named SniffLlama. The attack leverages a passive network monitoring strategy to observe the request–response timing of victim queries to LLMs deployed in school environments and infer sensitive attributes such as preferences and mental state of school children. The strategy is validated by deploying the LLM on a remote GPU (NVIDIA A100), where the attacker, the victim, and multiple benign users access the LLM through API calls. Our analysis shows that SniffLlama achieves a cache-hit recall of 100%, compared to 40% for EarlyBird, even in the presence of noise introduced by concurrent users. Overall, the attack accuracy of SniffLlama is 90.3%, compared to 52.3% for EarlyBird.